# Cozy Desktop Office

This repository contains a small Windows desktop app that creates a cozy pink mini-office scene behind desktop icons, while keeping normal desktop icons and the taskbar usable.

The app is intentionally beginner-friendly, uses local file storage only, and avoids destructive file operations.

## Why this architecture

This project uses a native Windows desktop host with a WPF rendering layer.

- WPF is a good fit for a beginner friendly Windows app because it is part of the default .NET desktop stack and supports gradients, shapes, animations, images, and simple UI controls.
- A native desktop host is needed because a normal borderless window is not enough to place content behind desktop icons. The app uses the standard Windows desktop shell integration pattern (Progman / WorkerW) to attach the scene to the actual desktop background layer.
- The app keeps the editor/customizer in a normal UI window, and the room scene can be displayed as a desktop host when enabled.
- All file copying is local, isolated to the user's machine, and never deletes the original files.

This is a practical and supported approach for Windows 10/11, and it is much easier for a beginner to maintain than building a custom engine from scratch.

## What is included

- Cozy room scene with a pink palette, desk, chair, window, bookshelf, bulletin board, drawers, rug, and plant decorations
- Day / Sunset / Night lighting with automatic local-clock transitions
- Local settings and saved room preferences
- Safe copying of files into real folders under Documents\CozyDesktopOffice
- Drag and drop support for dropping files onto the room surfaces
- Desktop mode toggle and startup option
- Honest desktop integration notes and fallback behavior
- Apple Music station UI that opens the official Apple Music page and does not fake playback data

## Folder structure

- `src/CozyDesktopOffice/` - WPF project source
- `README.md` - project summary and usage instructions

## Prerequisites

- Windows 10 or Windows 11
- Visual Studio 2022 with .NET desktop development workload
- .NET 8 SDK

## Build and run

Open a Developer PowerShell or cmd in the repository root and run:

```powershell
dotnet restore
 dotnet build src/CozyDesktopOffice/CozyDesktopOffice.csproj -c Debug
 dotnet run --project src/CozyDesktopOffice/CozyDesktopOffice.csproj
```

## Desktop mode testing

1. Run the application normally.
2. Click the button named `Desktop mode`.
3. Confirm the room appears behind the desktop icons.
4. Check the taskbar and desktop icons still work.
5. Use `Exit desktop mode` to return to the normal app window.

Important: this project follows the common Windows WorkerW pattern, but desktop integration is sensitive to the shell state and may vary by machine. If the shell does not accept the parent relationship, the app will show a fallback message and keep the regular window available.

## Safe file storage

Files are stored under:

```text
%USERPROFILE%\Documents\CozyDesktopOffice\
```

This includes:

- `Bookshelf/`
- `Desk/`
- `BulletinBoard/`
- `Drawers/`
- `settings/`

The app copies files instead of moving them. If a file already exists, a numbered file name is created instead of overwriting.

## Desktop integration and limitations

This implementation uses Win32 shell APIs to attach a windows host to the desktop's worker window. This is the standard desktop wallpaper integration technique used by custom desktop environments.

Known limitations:

- Windows shell behavior can vary on different PCs and with different desktop customization tools.
- Some third-party desktop tools may prevent the background layer from attaching reliably.
- Explorer drag-and-drop onto the desktop wallpaper is not guaranteed by Windows APIs, so the app uses visible in-room drop targets and file picker flows as a stable interaction path.

The app does not fake support. If desktop mode cannot be attached, the app remains usable in a normal window and tells you clearly why.

## Apple Music station

The project includes a pink Apple Music station panel. It opens the official Apple Music page and is deliberately honest about authorization requirements.

It does not fake currently playing music or pretend playback information exists without a proper Apple Music authorization flow. If Apple Music is not available or the OS does not provide playback metadata, the UI will show that status honestly.

## Daily lighting

The room updates automatically using the local system clock:

- Day: bright natural light
- Sunset: warm pink/orange glow
- Night: dim room with glowing desk lamp
- Morning: transitions back to daylight

You can also preview each mode manually.

## What this project delivers today

This is the smallest plausible working version of the concept:

- Room can render in a Windows app
- Desktop integration is implemented via the Windows shell host approach
- Local clock-driven lighting works
- Safe local file copying is implemented
- The user can add photos to the bulletin board and files to the desk and bookshelf
- The main office scene is built and can be customized

This is a realistic beginner project for Windows 10/11 and is designed so you can extend it without rewriting the whole app.

## Testing checklist

- Run the app with no errors
- Enable desktop mode and confirm icons remain clickable
- Drag a file onto the bookshelf or desk drop zones to copy it safely
- Check that the file lands in the real `Documents\CozyDesktopOffice` folders
- Open the app again and verify the room layout and settings persist
- Toggle between day, sunset, and night modes
- Disable desktop mode and confirm the app still works

## Notes

This repository is intentionally honest about the limitations of Windows desktop integration and Apple Music authorization. It avoids fake behavior and keeps all file handling local and safe.

